# env files — keep .env.example tracked
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.bak-batch*

# --- engine RUNTIME state — never committed (SKR-023) ---
# NOTE: .gitignore cannot untrack. 341 files under state/ are ALREADY TRACKED
# at HEAD (checkpoint.json, tasks.json, merge-queue.json, costs.json and 300
# task-updates/*.json), so the rules below do NOT stop those being committed
# again, and state/README.md's claim that checkpoint.json "is generated at
# runtime and not committed" is false today. Untracking them is a deliberate
# history decision (SPEC.md SKR-023 / BLK-4) and is deliberately NOT taken
# here — these rules keep the runtime paths ignored either way.
state/

# RUNTIME paths, repeated explicitly AFTER the blanket rule above. Git
# currently attributes these paths to the blanket state/ rule (an excluded
# directory short-circuits everything under it), so today they are belt and
# braces — but the blanket state/ entry is exactly what an untracking decision
# will narrow, and these are the paths that must stay ignored when it does.
#
# Written on every run: apps/cli/src/index.ts resolves the state root
# (MMCS_STATE_DIR, else repo-root state/) and opens MMCS_DB, the approval
# store and the human-review store there; scripts/orchestration/*.ts and
# scripts/hooks/*.ts take watchdog/merge/checkpoint locks under state/locks/.
# A lock or checkpoint that ships with a clone is not "no lock yet" — it is a
# lock owned by a process that does not exist in this container, so every
# waiter blocks for the whole stale window (15 min watchdog/merge, 60 s
# checkpoint) before anything can proceed. The SQLite sidecars are named
# individually: dropping -wal/-shm at the wrong instant corrupts a WAL db.
state/mmcs.db
state/mmcs.db-wal
state/mmcs.db-shm
state/locks/
state/approvals/
state/human-review/

# composite emit output + per-pkg dist links
packages/dist/
packages/*/dist
